      "content": "Wait, hold on. I just re-read this and I'm a bit concerned. \n\n\"Stochastically translocated via serendipitous AAV9 receptor tropism\"? That's not what we're doing at all. \"Serendipitous\" implies it's happening by chance/accident, and we're actually using a precision delivery system. Also, \"transfectin\" isn't even a word, and the part about dielectric barrier discharge seems way too over-complicated for a standard electroporation step.\n\nThe score shot up to 78, which is great for the auditor, but the actual science just got way too muddled and, frankly, sounds a bit fake. Can we dial it back? I need you to find a way to hit that 65-75 range with actual, accurate scientific terminology instead of just layering on complex-sounding words that don't actually describe the protocol correctly. Keep it rigorous, not just dense.",
